For the course project, your team will explore the machine learning life cycle: you will find a use case / application you are excited about, get a baseline machine learning model operational for that application, improve the model’s performance, and report your findings in a research conference paper format. 
In this motivation section of the project, we are exploring the “why”. Before we start any data processing or model programming, we need to identify the problem and what solutions exist. Then, we need to make a roadmap of how we will investigate and (hopefully!) solve the problem ourselves.

1.	Who is your target audience / end user?
	College football fans and analysts are the audience. 
	College football fans and analysts who want to use information to better understand and evaluate upcoming games. 
	The system is made for people who care about team performance and game predictions. 
	Who may not have the time or the statistical background to build predictive models themselves.

2.	What is the problem to solve? 
	We want to create a machine learning model that predicts who will win an NCAA Division I FBS football game using data that is known before the game starts.
	The model will look at team and game data like how well a team scores, how well they stop the other team how many times they lose the ball, how they do when running the ball and throwing the ball what happened in previous
	games and maybe rankings or other things that are known before the game starts—to figure out which team is more likely to win.

3.	Why is this an important problem that needs to be solved right now? How will solving it improve your end user’s life?
	College football generates a lot of stats every week. It can be hard for fans to know which of those numbers actually help predict what will happen in a game. Some systems already show that past performance data and machine learning can help find patterns between how teams play and who wins.
	Our project aims to give users a way to bring all these statistics into one prediction. Instead ff just using lines like win-loss records or average points scored people can use a model that looks at many parts of team performance at once.
	This could make things better for fans by:
		Giving a starting point to look at upcoming games with real numbers.
		Helping make big piles of football data easier to understand.
		Showing which team stats are most helpful when making predictions.
		Letting users compare the model’s guesses with results.

