8.	How will you measure success? (Examples: Classification: Accuracy, F1, ROC-AUC, Precision/Recall; Regression: MSE, RMSE, R²)
	Because our main task is classification our main evaluation metrics will be:
		Accuracy. The percentage of games where the model correctly predicts the winner.
		Precision. How often a predicted win for a team actually results in a win.
		Recall. How actual wins the model manages to detect.
		F1-score. A balance between precision and recall.
		ROC-AUC. Shows how well the model separates the two outcomes.
	Our main metric will probably be accuracy since the goal is simply to predict whether a team wins or loses.
	We will report several metrics instead of depending only on accuracy. This helps us understand how the model behaves across situations.
	We will also compare every machine learning model to our baseline. Success will mean showing improvement over that baseline on a test set we did not use during training. It’s not enough to reach a certain accuracy number.

9.	Do you have enough computational resources to handle the machine learning technique and the data? Can you train the model with the resources you have?
	Yes.
	The models and dataset that we have planned are not very large when compared to the datasets that are usually used for learning. Our first models can be trained on personal computers using Python and libraries.
	The College Football Data API gives game and statistical information through HTTP requests so we do not need special equipment to get the data.
	The dataset will include thousands of games not millions or billions of examples. Because of this our main challenge with computing is expected to be preparing the data and creating features, rather than the hardware needed for training the models.

